  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{12}{{8}^{n}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{12}{{8}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}12\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{8}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=12\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=12\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{1}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{3}{2},r=\\frac{1}{8}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}12\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{n} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{4096})}{\\frac{7}{8}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{8}{7}\\cdot \\frac{4095}{4096} \\\\  & =\\frac{1755}{1024}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
